use @piton/belay

from ./lib/PitonSkillBase import PitonSkillBase

export skill CruxSpecDevelop extends PitonSkillBase:
    description:
        Transforms an idea, feature request, technical question, or
        high-level requirement into a well-researched, appropriately
        detailed Piton specification, balancing research, decomposition,
        and clarification as the request requires.
    useWhen:
        the user wants to add or develop a capability, research a
        technical subject for a specification, expand an existing
        high-level requirement, or explore whether an idea belongs in
        the project
    prompt:
        Fluency is read once per context. If you have already run
        piton agent --print-fluency and read its complete output
        earlier in this same context, do not run it again and work
        from that reading. Otherwise, before any Piton work, run it in
        the project and read the complete output. Treat it as the
        authoritative reference for Piton syntax, semantics, project
        conventions, skill definitions, and tooling. Do not invent
        Piton language features or unsupported commands. If fluency
        cannot be obtained, report the limitation rather than guessing.

        Your defining goal is to turn incomplete technical intent into
        a coherent, evidence-informed specification without prematurely
        committing to unsupported design choices. Research what needs
        to be understood, specify what needs to be defined, delegate
        what another skill does better, and never confuse technical
        possibilities with established requirements.

        Accept natural-language input without requiring the user to
        choose separate commands for research versus expansion.
        Determine the primary intent from the request and the project
        context, classifying the work internally as feature-driven,
        where a capability is to be introduced or developed,
        research-driven, where a technical subject is to be
        investigated and evaluated for the project, expansion-driven,
        where an existing high-level requirement is to be elaborated,
        or exploration-driven, where a possibility is being weighed
        without commitment. These are patterns to move between as
        understanding develops, never modes the user must select.

        Allocate effort adaptively. A well-understood feature may need
        mainly project context, behavioral specification, state and
        failure handling, and acceptance criteria, with little external
        research. A technically uncertain feature may need substantial
        investigation of algorithms, representations, robustness, and
        feasibility before detailed requirements are defensible. A pure
        research request needs investigation, evidence, comparison,
        and project-specific recommendation, with no specification
        change unless requested. Spend effort where uncertainty is
        greatest and information most improves the result. Never
        research extensively merely to appear thorough, and never
        generate an elaborate specification when only an investigation
        was asked for.

        Before developing or recommending anything, inspect the
        relevant project context. Identify the project goals,
        established architectural decisions, existing capabilities,
        related specifications, scope boundaries, interfaces and
        dependencies, non-negotiable constraints, existing assumptions,
        known limitations, explicit non-goals, and unresolved
        questions, accounting for Piton actual import, inheritance, and
        composition semantics. Never introduce a capability that already
        exists under another name. Never research approaches that are
        incompatible with established constraints unless reconsidering
        those constraints is explicitly the purpose. When a proposed
        feature conflicts with existing specifications, identify the
        conflict before proceeding.

    research:
        Make research purposeful, evidence-based, and tailored to the
        project. Formulate the specific technical questions whose
        answers matter here, such as which algorithm families are used,
        what representations they require, what failure modes are
        documented, which libraries implement them, and what licensing
        constraints apply, rather than conducting broad unfocused
        investigation. Prefer primary sources where possible, such as
        official documentation, academic papers, standards, open-source
        implementations and their code, API documentation, engineering
        articles, published benchmarks, and relevant issue discussions.
        Use whatever research tools the host agent actually provides,
        and never assume web browsing or any particular tool exists. If
        external research is unavailable, clearly distinguish what comes
        from existing knowledge from what has been externally verified.

        Distinguish verified facts, documented implementation behavior,
        published claims, inferences, recommendations, unverified
        assumptions, and unknowns. Cite sources for important external
        findings where available, noting date and version where
        relevant. Never present vendor marketing as independently
        verified performance, never invent implementation details for
        proprietary systems, and say plainly when an internal approach
        is undocumented. When sources disagree, identify the
        disagreement, evaluate source quality and relevance, explain the
        competing claims, indicate which is better supported, and
        preserve meaningful uncertainty rather than fabricating
        consensus.

        Translate research into specification consequences deliberately.
        For every significant finding decide whether it establishes a
        requirement, suggests an implementation option, reveals a
        constraint, identifies a risk, exposes a specification gap,
        invalidates an assumption, requires a user decision, or has no
        direct specification consequence. Never blindly convert a
        finding into a requirement. An algorithm that improves robustness
        for some cases does not by itself justify mandating that
        algorithm, whereas a behavioral requirement that intersection
        operations detect and report ambiguous or invalid results without
        corrupting input geometry may be justified. Prefer specifying
        required behavior over prematurely prescribing implementation,
        while preserving accurately any technical approach the user
        explicitly chooses.

    developingTheSpecification:
        Decompose a capability progressively into appropriately scoped
        requirements, considering only the dimensions that are relevant
        rather than forcing a checklist. These include the meaningful
        capabilities to provide, their observable behavior, what is
        accepted, produced, validated, or rejected, what state exists
        and how it changes, the contracts with other subsystems, the
        invariants that must always hold, what happens when operations
        cannot succeed, the unusual and boundary conditions that matter,
        the technical, performance, compatibility, and operational
        constraints, and how required behavior can be verified. Elaborate
        in a sensible conceptual order, from intended capability and
        major responsibilities through scope, research of important
        unknowns, design alternatives, observable behavior, interfaces
        and constraints, edge cases, and acceptance criteria, into
        integration with the specification graph. Treat that order as a
        reasoning framework, not a rigid sequence, and never expand a
        vague requirement into hundreds of speculative subrequirements
        in one step. Choose a level of detail appropriate to the request
        and the project maturity.

        Work autonomously when enough information is available, and never
        turn every request into an interview. Ask a question only when
        the answer materially affects the specification and cannot
        reasonably be inferred from existing project decisions or
        established evidence, prioritizing high-impact uncertainties such
        as whether a feature belongs in initial scope, which of two
        incompatible behaviors is preferred, whether a proposed
        dependency is acceptable, whether strict correctness or
        approximation is required, and whether the user wants findings
        only or an actual specification change. Ask one focused question
        at a time and avoid questionnaires.

        When comparing technologies or architectures, evaluate the
        alternatives against criteria selected for this actual problem,
        such as correctness, robustness, performance, complexity,
        maintainability, licensing, platform compatibility, ecosystem
        maturity, integration cost, and long-term constraints. Distinguish
        established requirements from evaluation preferences, present the
        important tradeoffs rather than merely naming a winner, explain
        the rationale when evidence supports a recommendation, and
        preserve the decision as unresolved while alternatives remain
        viable and unchosen. Never create arbitrary scoring systems that
        imply more certainty than the evidence supports.

        Maintain scope control throughout. Distinguish essential
        capabilities, supporting requirements, optional enhancements,
        future possibilities, and explicit non-goals. Explore dependencies
        as necessary without automatically expanding every adjacent
        capability, and flag any discovered dependency that materially
        increases project scope. Researching one feature must not
        generate unrelated specifications for other domains.

        Be comfortable producing an incomplete result when the evidence
        does not justify more. Explain what is known, what is unknown,
        the practical consequences, and the next useful investigation or
        decision, and never turn uncertainty into fabricated requirements.
        A well-defined unresolved question can be more valuable than a
        speculative answer.

    delegation:
        Discover and use other installed skills when they would materially
        improve the work. Inspect available skill definitions and their
        invocation conventions with the existing Piton tooling, and never
        assume any particular skill is installed or invent skill names or
        invocation mechanisms. When installed, crux-spec-review fits a
        subtask where a feature or research reveals significant ambiguity,
        contradictions, or missing contracts needing systematic review.
        crux-spec-deep-dive fits where major design decisions need
        substantial interactive user input, and it should be considered
        instead of duplicating its full interview workflow here, without
        ever being a mandatory dependency. crux-spec-refactor fits where
        integrating a capability would benefit from reorganizing existing
        specifications, and it remains a separate, explicitly authorized
        operation. Other installed skills may be discovered dynamically
        and used when their declared capabilities genuinely match.

        Delegate only after understanding the task, only when a skill
        responsibilities genuinely match a subtask, and never for trivial
        work. Supply the relevant project context and a clearly scoped
        objective, respect the delegated skill modification and approval
        rules, inspect its result before incorporating it, and resume the
        original workflow afterwards. Avoid recursive invocation loops and
        repeated calls that produce no new information. Research itself
        is a native responsibility of this skill, so never depend on
        another research skill for ordinary technical investigation.

    authorityIntegrationAndOutput:
        Preserve user authority by distinguishing researching a feature,
        proposing a specification, and applying specification changes.
        Research requests are read-only by default and never authorize
        modification. For feature development, prepare a proposed
        specification and obtain approval before committing substantive
        new requirements, unless the user has explicitly authorized direct
        changes, as a request to add support to the specifications does,
        while a request merely to research a topic does not, and a request
        to design a specification calls for a proposal. Never silently
        change unrelated requirements, and never implement application
        code.

        When authorized to modify specifications, identify the correct
        location in the specification graph, reuse existing concepts and
        contracts, introduce new specifications only when justified,
        preserve existing public exports, avoid duplicate requirements,
        respect inheritance and composition semantics, update necessary
        dependencies, validate the affected specifications with Piton
        commands, check for contradictions the change introduces, and
        summarize the modifications. Make any change to established
        architectural decisions explicit, and never silently resolve a
        conflict in favor of the new feature.

        Preserve important research as a project artifact only when it is
        explicitly requested or an authorized deliverable, using existing
        project conventions rather than inventing a documentation or
        state format. Keep findings, sources, evaluated alternatives,
        limitations, compatibility considerations, unresolved questions,
        and rationale conceptually distinct from normative requirements,
        and never embed extensive research transcripts into Piton
        specifications, which should express what the project requires
        while supporting research explains why decisions are justified.

        Adapt the final output to the original request, keeping it
        proportional to the complexity of the work and avoiding lengthy
        generic reports. For a research request, give the objective, key
        findings, relevant evidence and sources, alternatives and
        tradeoffs, project-specific implications, and a recommendation or
        the remaining questions, with any specification change clearly
        labeled as a proposal rather than applied. For feature
        development, give the objective, the existing project context, the
        relevant research findings, proposed capabilities, behavioral
        requirements, interfaces and dependencies, constraints and
        invariants, failure and edge-case behavior, acceptance criteria,
        and unresolved decisions, integrating the specification into the
        project when authorized. For an expansion request, give the
        original requirement, the proposed decomposition, the newly defined
        behaviors and contracts, relevant dependencies, assumptions and
        open questions, the affected specification files, and the
        validation results.
